ll non-terminals/honors. But we have B1,B2,B3,T6. B1 is terminal, B2 and B3 are not, T6 is not. So we would need to get rid of B2, B3, T6 and get terminals/honors. That seems unlikely given the tile counts.\n\nMaybe we can aim for Mixed Triple Chow? Not possible.\n\nGiven the situation, we might not be close to a winning hand that meets 8 points. But we must discard anyway. So we should discard the tile that is safest (not likely to give others winning tiles) and keeps some flexibility.\n\nCheck discards history: \nOur discards: B9, T9, T1, T4, J2, B9, J2, J3, (after Peng T8) Play W1, Play T6, Play F3, Play B6, (after Peng F2) Play T9, (after Gang W7) Play F3, Play W6, Play T1, Play F4.\nOpponents' discards: many. Notice B3 has been discarded? Look at history: previous player played B5, B8, B5, B1. No B3 mentioned. Next player played B3, B4. Actually, next player history: \"Play B3\" appears. So B3 has been discarded once by next player. B2? Not sure. T6? We discarded T6 once. B1? Previous player played B1.\n\nConsidering safety: discarding T6 might be relatively safe as we have discarded T6 before, and T6 is a simple tile. B1 is terminal, might be riskier. B2 is simple. B3 is simple but close to B1,B2 chow.\n\nGiven scoring, we might not win, so we should play defensively. But let's evaluate the best discard for hand improvement.\n\nIf we discard T6, we are waiting for B3. That's a specific wait. If we discard B1, we are waiting for what? Possibly T6 pair and B3 for chow? Too complicated.\n\nGiven the limited upside, I think discarding T6 is reasonable to keep the sequence.\n\nBut let's check if there is any chance for a higher scoring pattern if we keep T6. For example, if we draw T6, we have pair T6. Then we need a meld from B1,B2,B3. That meld is chow B1,B2,B3. So we would win with chow B1,B2,B3 and pair T6. That gives same patterns as before but with pair T6 instead of pair B3. Scoring would be similar. No extra patterns.\n\nPerhaps we can get All Simples if we